e" "question/problem" "value" "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${E}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/state.json" "file_sha256" "b540b6345aea8f1ec84430e09e2c77b98a2f1e0bb7be114e09fdd2b9751eea72"} {"question" "q-131384" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write down $E$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $E$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${E}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{E}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/state.json" "file_sha256" "b540b6345aea8f1ec84430e09e2c77b98a2f1e0bb7be114e09fdd2b9751eea72"} {"question" "q-13