} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\,4 & \\,1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,-1 & \\,-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/state.json" "file_sha256" "b540b6345aea8f1ec84430e09e2c77b98a2f1e0bb7be114e09fdd2b9751eea72"} {"question" "q-43005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write down $Q$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $Q$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Q\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\,4 & \\,1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,-1 & \\,-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${Q}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Q}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/state.json" "file_sha256" "b540b6345aea8f1ec84430e09e2c77b98a2f1e0bb7be114e09fdd2b9751eea72"} {"question" "q-43005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a8