-capture/14080619/state.json" "file_sha256" "b540b6345aea8f1ec84430e09e2c77b98a2f1e0bb7be114e09fdd2b9751eea72"} {"question" "q-43004" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write down $V$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $V$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[V\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${V}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{V}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/state.json" "file_sha256" "b540b6345aea8f1ec84430e09e2c77b98a2f1e0bb7be114e09fdd2b9751eea72"} {"question" "q-43004" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a8001d243f786fcf7dc29b56b9689ddd6ffa0ca0841dedb27f7c113350df4c16"} {"question" "q-43004" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/state.json" "file_sha256" "b540b6345aea8f1ec84430e09e2c77b98a2f1e0bb7be114e09fdd2b9751eea72"} {"question" "q-43004" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/state.json" "file_sha256" "b540b6345aea8f1ec84430e09e2c77b98a2f1e0bb7be114e09fdd2b9751eea72"} {"question" "q-43004" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/state.json" "file_sha256" "b540b6345aea8f1ec84430e09e2c77b98a2f1e0bb7be114e09fdd2b9751eea72"} {"question" "q-31872" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The inverse of the matrix $J$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[J\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/state.json" "file_sha256" "b540b6345aea8f1ec84430e09e2c77b98a2f1e0bb7be114e09fdd2b9751eea72"} {"question" "q-31872" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write down $J$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $J$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[J\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${J}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{J}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{6} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/state.json" "file_sha256" "b540b6345aea8f1ec84430e09e2c77b98a2f1e0bb7be114e09fdd2b9751eea72"} {"question" "q-31872" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a8001d243f786fcf7dc29b56b9689ddd6ffa0ca0841dedb27f7c113350df4c16"} {"question" "q-31872" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/state.json" "file_sha256" "b540b6345aea8f1ec84430e09e2c77b98a2f1e0bb7be114e09fdd2b9751eea72"} {"question" "q-31872" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{R}_{2}:=3{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/state.json" "file_sha256" "b540b6345aea8f1ec84430e09e2c77b98a2f1e0bb7be114e09fdd2b9751eea72"} {"question" "q-31872" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/state.json" "file_sha256" "b540b6345aea8f1ec84430e09e2c77b98a2f1e0bb7be114e09fdd2b9751eea72"} {"question" "q-75448" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The inverse of the matrix $Y$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/state.json" "file_sha256" "b540b6345aea8f1ec84430e09e2c77b98a2f1e0bb7be114e09fdd2b9751eea72"} {"question" "q-75448" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write down $Y$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $Y$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${Y}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Y}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/state.json" "file_sha256" "b540b6345aea8f1ec84430e09e2c77b98a2f1e0bb7be114e09fdd2b9751eea72"} {"question" "q-75448" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a8001d243f786fcf7dc29b56b9689ddd6ffa0ca0841dedb27f7c113350df4c16"} {"question" "q-75448" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/state.json" "file_sha256" "b540b6345aea8f1ec84430e09e2c77b98a2f1e0bb7be114e09fdd2b9751eea72"} {"question" "q-75448" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{R}_{1}:=\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:=-2{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/state.json" "file_sha256" "b540b6345aea8f1ec84430e09e2c77b98a2f1e0bb7be114e09fdd2b9751eea72"} {"question" "q-75448" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/state.json" "file_sha256" "b540b6345aea8f1ec84430e09e2c77b98a2f1e0bb7be114e09fdd2b9751eea72"} {"question" "e-4725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2 \\,$ and $\\, R_2 :=  R_2+(-1)R_1?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/assets/e607bba88cc1f15abaa45fc3cf02110243433d60259c56805515ced373054ca0.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/example-4725.json" "file_sha256" "7b4d9573dbc2ce47d2da2eda5faf9c6dd03fe2e42a709568216861eb8eace40e"} {"question" "e-4725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/example-4725.json" "file_sha256" "7b4d9573dbc2ce47d2da2eda5faf9c6dd03fe2e42a709568216861eb8eace40e"} {"question" "e-4724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine $A^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/example-4724.json" "file_sha256" "b67b598b54e8cdf105fdde337f228e3f6f5b015af910918f06c8ce229464e53f"} {"question" "e-4724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/assets/89189584fab0cbb79fb0b4ac30a81b41bfd6508de7499c111fd53fb6c0c2c928.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/example-4724.json" "file_sha256" "b67b598b54e8cdf105fdde337f228e3f6f5b015af910918f06c8ce229464e53f"} {"question" "e-4723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The inverse of the matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/example-4723.json" "file_sha256" "313b4cd815a16394682d8f8ebd9235e1141dc193158ccc4f90b4f5c50137dcf2"} {"question" "e-4723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\nR_1 := \\dfrac{1}{2}R_1,\n\\qquad\nR_1 := R_1+(-3)R_2.\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/assets/0a034d98da82572a523d406379ab286d1af6b37820f5c2380822abf611643f83.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14080619/example-4723.json" "file_sha256" "313b4cd815a16394682d8f8ebd9235e1141dc193158ccc4f90b4f5c50137dcf2"}] "usage" {"q-75448" [] "q-130868" [] "q-43006" [] "q-131381" [] "e-4724" [] "q-31871" [] "q-43004" [] "e-4725" [] "q-131347" [] "q-43000" [] "e-4723" []} "basis" 2836 "retractions" [[17592186115763 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2 \\cdot 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations $R_{1}↔R_{2}$ and $R_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}R_{1}$?\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186115764 :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $A$ is found using the row operations as shown below. What are the corresponding row operations?\n\n$\\begin{bma